# =============================================================================
# braess.ogs - Braess's paradox, explained.
#
# This file is a ruleset. It holds no city of its own: it says what the roads
# and agents are. The map lives next to it, in braess.ogc. Open one and you
# get the other.
#
# WHAT BRAESS'S PARADOX IS
#
#   In 1968 Dietrich Braess proved something that feels wrong. If every driver
#   picks the fastest route for himself, adding a new road can make *everyone*
#   slower. The new link looks like a shortcut, so too many drivers take it,
#   it jams, and the average trip takes longer than before the road was built.
#
#   This ruleset exists to show that effect. It is not a city builder. There
#   are no zones, no budget, no growth: only a small road network and agents
#   that cross it.
#
# THE NETWORK IN braess.ogc
#
#   Four nodes, two main routes from Start to End, and one extra link in the
#   middle:
#
#        Start ---- Fast ---- node 2 ---- Slow ---- End
#                     \                    /
#                      \    Shortcut     /
#                       \                /
#        Start ---- Slow ---- node 3 ---- Fast ---- End
#
#   Without the shortcut the two routes are equal. With it, the router sends
#   agents on what looks fastest *right now* - the same selfish choice Braess
#   described. Raise the traffic until the shortcut fills up: total travel
#   time goes up even though a "better" road exists.
#
# WHAT TO WATCH
#
#   Open braess.ogc and look at the Traffic panel. At low traffic both main
#   routes share the load. As more agents leave Start, the shortcut turns red
#   on the heatmap first. The relative gap and the total travel time tell you
#   when the assignment has settled.
#
# IF YOU EDIT THIS FILE
#
#   A save records a fingerprint of the ruleset it was written against, and
#   refuses to open against a ruleset that changed since. Use Apply in the
#   Script panel of the demo: it reloads the rules and stamps the saves next
#   to this file with the new fingerprint.
# =============================================================================


resources
	resource People
end

paths
	path Road color 0xAAAAAA
end

# =============================================================================
# SEGMENTS - three kinds of road, chosen so the paradox is easy to see.
#
#   Fast      high speed, low capacity  - good when empty, bad when full
#   Slow      low speed, high capacity  - never fast, but it absorbs a crowd
#   Shortcut  very high speed, very low capacity - the link that should help
#               and ends up hurting once enough agents use it
#
# speed, capacity and beta are what the router uses to pick an itinerary. It
# does not look for the shortest road, but for the fastest one *now*.
# =============================================================================
segments
	segment Fast color 0x55CC55 speed 80 capacity 8 beta 4
	segment Slow color 0xCC5555 speed 20 capacity 40 beta 4
	segment Shortcut color 0xCCCC55 speed 200 capacity 6 beta 4
end

agents
	agent Worker color 0xFFFFFF speed 40
end

rules

	# Start releases one agent every four ticks. Each one is a Worker looking
	# for End and carrying one People. Turn the rate down to send fewer agents
	# and watch the paradox appear only at higher traffic.
	buildingRule Send
		rate 4
		local People remove 1
		agent Worker to End add [ People 1 ]
	end

	# End removes each traveller once they arrive. Without this rule agents
	# would pile up at the destination and the network would stop moving.
	buildingRule Absorb
		rate 1
		local People greater 0
		local People remove 1
	end
end

# =============================================================================
# BUILDINGS - Start sends agents out, End absorbs them. Nothing else happens
# on the map: the whole point is to watch traffic on the roads between them.
# =============================================================================
buildings
	building Start color 0x00FF00 layerRadius 1 rules [ Send ] targets [ Start ] caps [ People 200 ] resources [ People 80 ]
	building End color 0xFF0000 layerRadius 1 rules [ Absorb ] targets [ End ] caps [ People 200 ] resources [ ]
end

layers
	layer People color 0xFFFF00 capacity 10 rules [ ]
end
